  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nOur \"rags to riches\" turnaround in rainfall should continue late \nthis week into next week across the Panhandles, as models \npersistently indicate an active weather pattern. Increasing lift \nwithin south-southwesterly flow aloft ahead of the approaching \ntrough should maintain the influx of enhanced tropical moisture \n(PWATs 1.0-1.5\"), generating widespread showers and embedded weak \nstorms for the Panhandles Friday - Fri night. Rain should gradually \ndiminish through the overnight hours into Saturday, as the passage \nof the upper level trough axis provides a push of drier mid-level \nair. Current probabilities for measurable rain remain quite \npromising across the Panhandles, as there are very few reasons for \nconcern to be found (60-90% POPs area wide). In general though, \nconfidence in receiving beneficial rainfall by Saturday morning is \nhighest to the southeast (40-70% chance for >1\" rainfall), and lower \nfor the northwest (10-40% chance for >0.5\" rainfall). \n\nIf you don't see much rain from this first system, global models \nshow additional chances Sunday night into Monday as another deep \ntrough approaches from the west. This system is projected to have \nanother strong push of moisture from the south being advected to the \nregion, which spells 30-60% rain probabilities for a majority of the \nCWA. However, there does appear to be greater uncertainty with this \nsystem, as models disagree on a few synoptic features. These \ndisagreements primarily hinge on differing tracks and timing of \nthe trough, and potential for a dry slot to infiltrate the region \nbefore best lift arrives (like the GFS currently shows). \nRegardless, it is likely that there will be at least a brief \nwindow for showers and storms at some point Sunday night - \nMonday.\n\nTemperatures through the Fri-Mon time frame look to stay quite \nseasonal in the 60s and 70s, with overnight lows in the 40s and 50s. \nWe can anticipate a strong cold front to charge over the Panhandles \nMonday as stout northwest flow aloft on the backside of the latter \ntrough passage stretches over the region. Rapid pressure rises and \nstacked jet dynamics behind this FROPA spells a solidly windy start \nto the week Mon & Tue, with sustained northwest winds of 20-30 mph \ngusting 40 mph or possibly greater both days. This will aid cold air \nadvection to the southern High Plains, but will impact overnight \nlows more than afternoon highs (lows in the 30s and 40s). Model \nagreement on synoptic evolution further fractures Tuesday and \nbeyond, but our active pattern may linger through much of next week.\n\nHarrel",
